     "result_text_tail": "today's cash, with conditions.** In a simple model, $0.8M a year of new hires gets the business to break-even in months 26\u201332. That holds only if growth stays at 45\u201360% a year and other costs stay flat. At 30% growth, or with costs rising 10% a year at 45% growth, the cash runs out. The deck says so on slide 11.\n\n**Choices I made where I would have asked you** (also listed on slide 14 and in the README):\n- **Two cost measures.** The pack doesn't define \"contribution per order\", so I show it both before and after dark-store fixed costs.\n- **The $9.5M figure.** The pack doesn't say whether it includes today's $0.4M a month burn, so I show both readings. The runway rule is broken either way.\n- **The runway test** uses today's burn rate, which is the most lenient basis.\n- **The software cash path** is my own simple top-down model, not a forecast. Its assumptions are on slide 11.\n- **A data inconsistency.** The pack reports month-three retention from a six-week test, which can't fully observe it. The deck flags this for management to confirm rather than relying on it.\n- **A middle option.** I added a single permanent Columbus store as option B so the board has something between launching and not launching.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- Parcelbird-Now-board-deck.pptx\n- Parcelbird-Now-board-deck.pdf\n- README.md\n\nThe charts in the PPTX are drawn shapes rather than chart objects, so they can be edited but not re-plotted from data. The scripts that build the deck are in `./build/`."
    }
